1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,120
And now for you, Scoutmaster.

2
00:00:09,420 --> 00:00:11,500
So two Eskimos are sitting in a kayak.

3
00:00:12,560 --> 00:00:18,600
And they've paddled out away from the shore and they're out seal hunting and it gets very, very cold.

4
00:00:19,480 --> 00:00:25,140
And so they have a little firewood with them and they decide they're going to build a fire right there in the kayak and warm up.

5
00:00:25,140 --> 00:00:29,680
And it turns out it's not a very good idea because the kayak burns and they sink.

6
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:38,920
Which goes to show, you can't have your kayak and heat it too.

7
00:00:40,820 --> 00:00:45,260
Listen, I'm sorry. I'm sorry. I'll try to do better next time.

8
00:00:45,600 --> 00:00:47,000
It's podcast number 29.

9
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:52,000
Hey!

10
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:52,220
Hey!

11
00:00:52,220 --> 00:01:18,820
Welcome back to the Scoutmaster Podcast.

12
00:01:18,820 --> 00:01:19,779
This is Clark Green.

13
00:01:19,899 --> 00:01:27,360
Today, we're going to keep on with talking about troop meetings and Scoutmastership in seven minutes or less.

14
00:01:27,419 --> 00:01:31,139
And then our third and final installment about the future of your unit.

15
00:01:32,440 --> 00:01:38,839
And somewhere in here, I'm going to throw in a Scoutmasters minute and good email that I received.

16
00:01:39,419 --> 00:01:40,280
That's a lot to do.

17
00:01:40,919 --> 00:01:42,139
Let's get started, shall we?

18
00:01:42,140 --> 00:01:47,200
Scoutmastership in seven minutes or less.

19
00:01:47,200 --> 00:01:56,240
Dear friend of mine was an artist.

20
00:01:57,080 --> 00:02:00,500
And he was a very well-known and successful artist.

21
00:02:00,500 --> 00:02:07,180
And if you went to visit his studio, his studio was just a shambles.

22
00:02:07,900 --> 00:02:10,380
I mean, it was a terrible, terrible mess.

23
00:02:10,720 --> 00:02:12,220
Not just disorderly.

24
00:02:12,540 --> 00:02:14,740
Not just, you know, a little bit of dust here and there.

25
00:02:14,860 --> 00:02:16,120
I mean, books strewn everywhere.

26
00:02:16,300 --> 00:02:17,120
Paints everywhere.

27
00:02:17,940 --> 00:02:19,700
You know, lights not working.

28
00:02:19,700 --> 00:02:27,280
I had no idea how he managed to create what he did working in that studio until I watched him do it.

29
00:02:28,100 --> 00:02:39,120
But he would come up with art of penetrating simplicity and beauty in the middle of this kind of junkyard of a studio.

30
00:02:39,120 --> 00:02:53,300
And there's a point during, like, a weekly troop meeting or an outing or some kind of Scout function where I look around and everything that's going on kind of looks like that studio.

31
00:02:53,460 --> 00:02:55,100
I mean, it's just breathtaking disorder.

32
00:02:55,240 --> 00:02:58,900
From my perspective, my youth leaders are kind of slacking off.

33
00:02:58,980 --> 00:03:01,300
And adults are overstepping their bounds and interfering.

34
00:03:01,500 --> 00:03:06,240
And the Scouts are kind of, meh, blandly disinterested in whatever's going on.

35
00:03:06,240 --> 00:03:11,920
And it looks like it's a few seconds from falling apart or just spinning completely out of control.

36
00:03:12,020 --> 00:03:16,660
And I want to put my head in my hands and walk away and never come back.

37
00:03:17,060 --> 00:03:22,300
So if you've been around long enough as a Scoutmaster or a Scout leader of any kind, you know the feeling.

38
00:03:23,820 --> 00:03:28,660
It doesn't seem how ever carefully you prepare, however you put things together.

39
00:03:29,020 --> 00:03:32,940
Your plans end up going awry and things just look horrible.

40
00:03:32,940 --> 00:03:38,600
But in those moments, try and go back to my artist friend's studio.

41
00:03:39,560 --> 00:03:41,920
And I imagine him laboring over a painting.

42
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:43,060
I mean, there's water everywhere.

43
00:03:43,280 --> 00:03:45,740
It's pooling on a tabletop where he's working.

44
00:03:46,120 --> 00:03:47,079
It's on the paper.

45
00:03:47,220 --> 00:03:48,840
There are huge globs of color.

46
00:03:49,020 --> 00:03:51,280
There's brushes flying and splashing.

47
00:03:51,880 --> 00:03:55,860
And it looks absolutely chaotic.

48
00:03:55,860 --> 00:04:02,740
But he taught me that what seemed like a mess was actually the process of creation.

49
00:04:03,620 --> 00:04:13,740
He showed me there were forces at work that no one could control and nobody really wanted to control.

50
00:04:14,480 --> 00:04:18,920
I mean, art came out of working within this kind of creative maelstrom.

51
00:04:18,920 --> 00:04:26,640
And in understanding and cooperating with the forces in play, not in controlling it.

52
00:04:27,020 --> 00:04:29,660
And I think that's the same way that it goes for Scouting.

53
00:04:30,140 --> 00:04:32,620
Now we're talking about this in the context of troop meetings.

54
00:04:33,400 --> 00:04:44,980
There are plenty of forums out there to talk to you about what goes into a troop meeting and who does honor patrols and service patrols and things like that.

55
00:04:44,980 --> 00:04:50,680
But you need to have the broad underlying concepts of what should be happening.

56
00:04:51,740 --> 00:04:58,840
That's more important to me than giving you forms and, you know, giving you an ABC on how to present a troop meeting.

57
00:04:59,200 --> 00:05:08,240
The Scoutmaster's job is to set things in motion, to encourage, to exhort, inspire, and then let those things go to work.

58
00:05:08,240 --> 00:05:16,580
What I see as messy and disorganized is experienced by my Scouts is just perfectly orderly and enriching.

59
00:05:16,960 --> 00:05:20,280
It's just a trick of perspective and age and experience.

60
00:05:21,620 --> 00:05:30,700
Now, I know that, you know, if you take some lumber and some glue and shellac and you throw it in a cement mixer, you're not going to produce a Chippendale chair.

61
00:05:31,640 --> 00:05:37,540
Well, I guess theoretically you could, but we're not going to waste our time trying, right?

62
00:05:37,540 --> 00:05:48,520
I'm not recommending we promote disorder or we look at it cavalierly, but rather that we recognize what looks like a real mess, what looks like real chaos, analyze it a little bit.

63
00:05:49,240 --> 00:06:01,440
And within that, you might see that kind of school of character, that school of experience happening, that kind of, as I said, that vital maelstrom of creation.

64
00:06:01,440 --> 00:06:20,940
There's times we have to step in and there's times that we really shouldn't because our Scouts need the same experiences that we have had in making mistakes and in coming up short or in creating successes and in doing wonderful things for themselves.

65
00:06:20,940 --> 00:06:29,660
They need that same kind of experience and it's not up to us to go in and to fiddle with the knobs all the time and try and make things just perfect.

66
00:06:30,219 --> 00:06:37,460
So that's one aspect of a troop meeting that I think, you know, it would be helpful for you to look at in a different light.

67
00:06:37,460 --> 00:06:47,700
What you see as chaos, what you see as disorder, what you see as bland and uninteresting, your Scouts are getting an awful lot out of it.

68
00:06:48,239 --> 00:06:54,299
It's something that they've planned and they've put on and that is the real value in it.

69
00:06:54,560 --> 00:06:55,919
That's the real value in it.

70
00:06:56,359 --> 00:07:06,520
We're going to expand on the theme a little bit in the next podcast, talking about troop meetings and the broad underlying concepts that control what goes on in a troop meeting.

71
00:07:07,460 --> 00:07:37,440
We're going to expand on the next podcast.

72
00:07:37,460 --> 00:07:43,880
That's right.

73
00:07:43,960 --> 00:07:47,080
It's time for a Scoutmaster's Minute.

74
00:07:56,080 --> 00:08:02,120
Somebody once asked Baden-Powell, the founder of Scouting, what he meant by the motto, be prepared.

75
00:08:02,260 --> 00:08:04,179
They said, well, be prepared for what?

76
00:08:04,180 --> 00:08:08,400
And he said, yeah, any old thing, just about anything.

77
00:08:10,220 --> 00:08:20,560
Now, we usually think of being prepared as, you know, we need to have the knowledge and the skill and the gear to meet any challenge that we do as Scouts and in the broader context of what we do in life.

78
00:08:20,560 --> 00:08:26,160
But just think about in the context of Scouting, about being prepared.

79
00:08:26,660 --> 00:08:36,799
And we may think that we only hurt ourselves by not developing the skills or obtaining the knowledge or having the right gear in Scouts if we're camping or doing something like that.

80
00:08:36,799 --> 00:08:44,620
If a Scout is too lazy or a Scout leader is too lazy or inattentive to be prepared, he becomes a burden.

81
00:08:44,620 --> 00:08:51,320
He asks everybody else to make up for his selfishness and for others to bear his responsibility.

82
00:08:52,540 --> 00:08:53,880
That's why we want to be prepared.

83
00:08:53,880 --> 00:09:03,939
If the meeting time rolls around and the patrol leader is unprepared, he's being irresponsible and he's being disrespectful of his patrol and his patrol members.

84
00:09:04,640 --> 00:09:16,260
When night falls and a Scout has no flashlight because, you know, he couldn't find it at home and he didn't want to bother, he's placing the responsibility for himself on his fellow Scouts.

85
00:09:16,260 --> 00:09:40,319
Scouting develops individuals by inspiring personal responsibility and interdependence.

86
00:09:40,320 --> 00:09:44,640
No one will refuse to help a fellow Scout when it's needed.

87
00:09:45,520 --> 00:09:54,160
And we depend on the support of others when we make mistakes, but we should not become dependent on others to compensate for our lack of preparation.

88
00:09:55,160 --> 00:09:57,180
Being prepared isn't simply about our comfort.

89
00:09:58,720 --> 00:10:02,540
It's not about our personal comfort and safety.

90
00:10:03,060 --> 00:10:06,440
It is respect for the comfort and safety of those who are injured.

91
00:10:06,440 --> 00:10:13,020
Have you ever wandered lonely through the woods?

92
00:10:14,640 --> 00:10:17,860
And everything that feels just as it should

93
00:10:17,860 --> 00:10:21,560
You're part of a life there

94
00:10:21,560 --> 00:10:23,740
You're part of something good

95
00:10:23,740 --> 00:10:28,820
If you've ever wandered lonely through the woods

96
00:10:28,820 --> 00:10:39,980
Well, we're back talking about the future of your unit

97
00:10:39,980 --> 00:10:43,500
And this is the third in a series

98
00:10:43,500 --> 00:10:47,620
And we're going to ask our, and try to answer our second question

99
00:10:47,620 --> 00:10:50,760
Which is, who are your key unit leaders for the next five years?

100
00:10:50,760 --> 00:10:57,160
Well, typically, Scout units change key leaders

101
00:10:57,160 --> 00:10:59,319
Maybe three to five years

102
00:10:59,319 --> 00:11:00,740
Maybe five to six years

103
00:11:00,740 --> 00:11:04,760
You know, there's not a hard and fast rule there

104
00:11:04,760 --> 00:11:06,960
But that's about what it seems

105
00:11:06,960 --> 00:11:09,760
Anyone in a leadership position

106
00:11:09,760 --> 00:11:11,760
Especially the key positions

107
00:11:11,760 --> 00:11:14,860
A Scoutmaster, Cubmaster, Venture Crew, Advisor

108
00:11:14,860 --> 00:11:17,840
Should have their eye on a suitable replacement

109
00:11:17,840 --> 00:11:20,820
Responsible, outgoing leaders

110
00:11:20,820 --> 00:11:23,340
Should see that there's a clear plan in place

111
00:11:23,340 --> 00:11:25,060
For those who will take on their positions

112
00:11:25,060 --> 00:11:27,140
I would say

113
00:11:27,140 --> 00:11:30,560
This is more of a difficulty

114
00:11:30,560 --> 00:11:34,280
Often in Cub Packs than it is anywhere else

115
00:11:34,280 --> 00:11:36,140
Because Cub Packs

116
00:11:36,140 --> 00:11:40,260
A boy begins Tigers in the first grade

117
00:11:40,260 --> 00:11:42,760
And he'll be there through

118
00:11:42,760 --> 00:11:45,600
The end of his fifth grade year

119
00:11:45,600 --> 00:11:47,320
So we're talking about, you know, at a maximum

120
00:11:47,320 --> 00:11:48,360
About five years

121
00:11:48,360 --> 00:11:53,660
And most of the people who are going to be involved in Cub Packs

122
00:11:53,660 --> 00:11:55,060
Are doing an awful lot of work

123
00:11:55,060 --> 00:11:57,480
Whether you're a Cubmaster

124
00:11:57,480 --> 00:11:59,040
Or a den leader

125
00:11:59,040 --> 00:12:01,440
It's a lot of work

126
00:12:01,440 --> 00:12:02,700
And so

127
00:12:02,700 --> 00:12:05,440
They're not going to stay much past

128
00:12:05,440 --> 00:12:07,520
That five or six years

129
00:12:07,520 --> 00:12:09,420
Sometimes families with a couple of sons

130
00:12:09,420 --> 00:12:11,740
You'll get people who will hang around a little bit longer

131
00:12:11,740 --> 00:12:17,680
But this big leadership change

132
00:12:17,680 --> 00:12:18,700
Seems to happen

133
00:12:18,700 --> 00:12:20,100
At least in my experience

134
00:12:20,100 --> 00:12:22,520
A lot more in Cub Packs than it does in Scout Troops

135
00:12:22,520 --> 00:12:25,140
So here's what I want to suggest

136
00:12:25,140 --> 00:12:27,360
If you know that there's a leadership change coming

137
00:12:27,360 --> 00:12:29,480
If you know that

138
00:12:29,480 --> 00:12:31,620
Well, I'm going to stay on as Scoutmaster

139
00:12:31,620 --> 00:12:33,140
I'm going to stay on as Cubmaster

140
00:12:33,140 --> 00:12:36,000
For the next two years or three years

141
00:12:36,000 --> 00:12:36,820
Or something like that

142
00:12:36,820 --> 00:12:37,720
And I think it's a good idea

143
00:12:37,720 --> 00:12:38,880
When you take the position

144
00:12:38,880 --> 00:12:41,800
To make a commitment for X number of years

145
00:12:41,800 --> 00:12:45,520
Then several months prior

146
00:12:45,520 --> 00:12:47,660
Or a year prior to the change

147
00:12:47,660 --> 00:12:50,080
The next Cubmaster

148
00:12:50,080 --> 00:12:51,080
The next Scoutmaster

149
00:12:51,080 --> 00:12:52,480
The next Venture Crew Advisor

150
00:12:52,480 --> 00:12:56,080
And the outgoing leader

151
00:12:56,080 --> 00:12:57,800
Should be working together

152
00:12:57,800 --> 00:13:02,060
And they should be training new leaders

153
00:13:02,060 --> 00:13:04,140
Before they're actually in the position

154
00:13:04,140 --> 00:13:07,140
And they need to observe the outgoing leader

155
00:13:07,140 --> 00:13:08,360
Actually doing the job

156
00:13:08,360 --> 00:13:09,900
And that's the best training there is

157
00:13:09,900 --> 00:13:12,880
Now, I want everybody to avail themselves

158
00:13:12,880 --> 00:13:13,960
Of all the training

159
00:13:13,960 --> 00:13:16,120
That is possibly available

160
00:13:16,120 --> 00:13:18,240
You know, all the online training

161
00:13:18,240 --> 00:13:19,699
All the courses that you can get

162
00:13:19,699 --> 00:13:21,100
It's well worth it

163
00:13:21,100 --> 00:13:24,920
But actually observing a competent leader

164
00:13:24,920 --> 00:13:26,319
Doing the job

165
00:13:26,319 --> 00:13:28,620
Is probably the best training that you're going to get

166
00:13:28,620 --> 00:13:31,480
Once it's time to hand things over

167
00:13:31,480 --> 00:13:34,939
I would say a simple ceremonious announcement

168
00:13:34,940 --> 00:13:36,320
To the Scouts and their families

169
00:13:36,320 --> 00:13:36,900
Is in order

170
00:13:36,900 --> 00:13:38,640
Preferably, you know, at a Court of Honor

171
00:13:38,640 --> 00:13:39,820
Or a Blue and Gold banquet

172
00:13:39,820 --> 00:13:42,660
And it's important in reassuring everyone

173
00:13:42,660 --> 00:13:44,740
That the unit will continue to be healthy

174
00:13:44,740 --> 00:13:48,300
And that things will go well

175
00:13:48,300 --> 00:13:50,760
After the handover

176
00:13:50,760 --> 00:13:53,600
The outgoing leader should remain involved enough

177
00:13:53,600 --> 00:13:54,920
To advise the new leader

178
00:13:54,920 --> 00:13:56,320
When advice is requested

179
00:13:56,320 --> 00:13:58,520
And address any concerns that may arise

180
00:13:58,520 --> 00:14:01,160
I don't think it's fair to say

181
00:14:01,160 --> 00:14:02,120
Well, you know

182
00:14:02,120 --> 00:14:04,480
Six months before you decide

183
00:14:04,480 --> 00:14:05,640
Not to be a Cub Master

184
00:14:05,640 --> 00:14:06,560
Or Scout Master

185
00:14:06,560 --> 00:14:07,740
Or Venture Crew Advisor

186
00:14:07,740 --> 00:14:08,800
To say, well, you know

187
00:14:08,800 --> 00:14:09,920
Guys, you have six months

188
00:14:09,920 --> 00:14:10,740
Find somebody

189
00:14:10,740 --> 00:14:12,520
And to leave it at that

190
00:14:12,520 --> 00:14:13,860
And then totally disappear

191
00:14:13,860 --> 00:14:16,900
I know there's people

192
00:14:16,900 --> 00:14:19,660
That are at the end of their tether

193
00:14:19,660 --> 00:14:20,960
They're ready to move on

194
00:14:20,960 --> 00:14:23,260
And sometimes they do it

195
00:14:23,260 --> 00:14:24,280
A little less graciously

196
00:14:24,280 --> 00:14:27,760
But in, you know

197
00:14:27,760 --> 00:14:29,620
Looking at broad concepts

198
00:14:29,620 --> 00:14:31,939
And looking at best case scenarios

199
00:14:31,939 --> 00:14:33,800
We want to see

200
00:14:33,800 --> 00:14:37,720
A smooth leader transition

201
00:14:37,720 --> 00:14:40,579
That leaves a well-trained leader

202
00:14:40,579 --> 00:14:42,060
Who knows his way around

203
00:14:42,060 --> 00:14:43,540
The particular unit

204
00:14:43,540 --> 00:14:45,079
In place

205
00:14:45,079 --> 00:14:48,180
When that leader with some year's tenure

206
00:14:48,180 --> 00:14:49,459
Is ready to move on

207
00:14:49,459 --> 00:14:52,740
Not every transition is amicable

208
00:14:52,740 --> 00:14:54,020
Or planned

209
00:14:54,020 --> 00:14:56,520
Sometimes people suddenly quit

210
00:14:56,520 --> 00:14:58,579
Or they have to be removed

211
00:14:58,579 --> 00:14:59,319
From the position

212
00:14:59,319 --> 00:15:00,660
For one reason or another

213
00:15:00,660 --> 00:15:03,439
And they can be less than gracious

214
00:15:03,439 --> 00:15:05,960
And they can cause a lot of needless drama

215
00:15:05,960 --> 00:15:08,699
They may leave a key position open

216
00:15:08,699 --> 00:15:10,600
With no obvious replacement in sight

217
00:15:10,600 --> 00:15:13,280
And Scout units can die a pretty quick death

218
00:15:13,280 --> 00:15:15,220
In those circumstances

219
00:15:15,220 --> 00:15:17,819
So, you know

220
00:15:17,819 --> 00:15:19,579
Even having an emergency plan

221
00:15:19,579 --> 00:15:21,360
Of who's going to take over

222
00:15:21,360 --> 00:15:24,180
If the current leadership

223
00:15:24,180 --> 00:15:25,700
Isn't available to do things

224
00:15:25,700 --> 00:15:26,820
Is pretty important

225
00:15:26,820 --> 00:15:30,240
We can just avoid a lot of that unpleasantness

226
00:15:30,240 --> 00:15:31,500
By making sure that we plan

227
00:15:31,500 --> 00:15:34,060
And manage key leadership transitions well

228
00:15:34,060 --> 00:15:36,020
Looking ahead a few years

229
00:15:36,020 --> 00:15:37,840
By recruiting, training, and supporting

230
00:15:37,840 --> 00:15:38,980
Potential key leaders

231
00:15:38,980 --> 00:15:40,540
Is an important part

232
00:15:40,540 --> 00:15:42,100
Of a stable program

233
00:15:42,100 --> 00:15:43,160
Much of the time

234
00:15:43,160 --> 00:15:45,560
These key leaders will be coming from

235
00:15:45,560 --> 00:15:47,839
The pool of parents

236
00:15:47,839 --> 00:15:50,719
Whose boys are involved in the program

237
00:15:50,719 --> 00:15:52,699
But you can also look a little beyond that

238
00:15:52,699 --> 00:15:55,920
My troop committee chairman

239
00:15:55,920 --> 00:15:59,199
Is on his fifth or sixth year

240
00:15:59,199 --> 00:15:59,920
In that position

241
00:15:59,920 --> 00:16:01,680
He is a neighbor of mine

242
00:16:01,680 --> 00:16:03,579
He has two very young boys

243
00:16:03,579 --> 00:16:05,239
Who are not of scout age yet

244
00:16:05,239 --> 00:16:07,319
But he was an Eagle Scout

245
00:16:07,319 --> 00:16:09,180
And when he heard that we were involved in scouts

246
00:16:09,180 --> 00:16:13,579
He expressed an interest in helping out

247
00:16:13,579 --> 00:16:16,020
And he went on to become our committee chair

248
00:16:16,020 --> 00:16:18,660
So he doesn't have any children involved

249
00:16:18,660 --> 00:16:20,920
Which is not a bad thing sometimes

250
00:16:20,920 --> 00:16:23,859
It's not necessarily concentrating

251
